```
sbi->s_sys_blocksize = be32_to_cpu(omfs_sb->s_sys_blocksize);
// missing: if (sbi->s_sys_blocksize < OMFS_DIR_START) ...
memset(bh->b_data + OMFS_DIR_START, 0, sbi->s_sys_blocksize - OMFS_DIR_START);
```
Reason:
The superblock value `s_sys_blocksize` is read from disk without a lower-bound check. When it is smaller than `OMFS_DIR_START` (440), the subsequent subtraction in `omfs_make_empty()` produces an unsigned integer underflow, resulting in a huge positive length. This drives a ~4 GiB `memset()` that corrupts kernel memory beyond the intended buffer.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (sbi->s_sys_blocksize < OMFS_DIR_START) {
    printk(KERN_ERR "omfs: sysblock size (%d) is too small\n",
            sbi->s_sys_blocksize);
    goto out_brelse_bh;
}
```
Reason:
The added validation rejects any superblock where `s_sys_blocksize` is smaller than `OMFS_DIR_START`. This prevents the underflow in `omfs_make_empty()` by failing early during superblock parsing, before any filesystem operations that depend on this value are performed.

